
[38;5;231m●[39m I created [38;5;153m/root/projects/hello/hello.html[39m.

  [1mHow to open it[0m

  Find the file on your computer and double-click it. It opens in your web browser. You can
  also right-click it and choose "Open with" and then Chrome, Firefox, or Safari.

  [1mWhat each part does[0m

  - [1m[38;5;153m<!DOCTYPE html>[0m tells the browser "this is a web page."
  - [1m[38;5;153m<html> ... </html>[0m wraps the whole page.
  - [1m[38;5;153m<head>[0m holds information about the page that isn't shown on it. Here that is the
    [38;5;153m<title>[39m, the text on the browser tab ("Hello Sam").
  - [1m[38;5;153m<body>[0m holds everything you actually see on the page.
  - [1m[38;5;153m<h1>Hello, Sam!</h1>[0m is the big heading that greets you.
  - [1m[38;5;153m<p id="date"></p>[0m is an empty paragraph with the name "date". It's a blank spot reserved
    for the date.
  - [1m[38;5;153m<script> ... </script>[0m holds a small program that runs when the page loads:
    - [38;5;153mnew Date()[39m asks the computer for the current date and time.
    - [38;5;153mgetElementById("date")[39m finds the blank paragraph by its name.
    - [38;5;153mtextContent = ...[39m writes "Today is" plus the nicely formatted date into that
      paragraph, for example "Tuesday, October 6, 2026".

  The date is worked out each time you open the page, so it's always today's date.

